[
    {
        "content": "<p>I know that there are members of our community who are very much interested in the understanding the theory behind pull-requests to Lean projects etc.. Given this I was wondering if anyone has looked at something along the lines of a logical system to changes (diffs) in the Lean environment: </p>\n<p>One part of the system might be that making changes which are  strictly independent of one another should add <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_1 \\oplus C_2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⊕</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. An example could be adding two lemmas which do not rely on each other. </p>\n<p>Another part of the system might be to signify changes which must follow one another e.g. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟹</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_1 \\implies C_2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⟹</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, meaning <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> must follow <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. An example could be adding two lemmas where the second uses the first. </p>\n<p>If it was practical to work out the structure of the changes in a given pull request, it might help in reviewing and breaking down large PRs.</p>",
        "id": 579221585,
        "sender_full_name": "Joseph Tooby-Smith",
        "timestamp": 1773394754
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Are you looking for the theory of how diffing algorithms work?</p>",
        "id": 579225663,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1773395933
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or asking for semantic diffs?</p>",
        "id": 579225763,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1773395962
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think semantic diffs. Basically, I'm asking can you utilise the Lean environment itself to get a better handle of the diffs and work out which changes are independent of each other, which need to follow each other, which need to be made all at once etc.</p>",
        "id": 579226427,
        "sender_full_name": "Joseph Tooby-Smith",
        "timestamp": 1773396150
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"243562\">@Adam Topaz</span> is working on tooling to build things like this. In particular, I hope that the \"Declarations diff\" in eg <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36579#issuecomment-4052142680\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36579#issuecomment-4052142680</a> will soon be computed deterministically using Lean, instead of grepping through the git diff.</p>",
        "id": 579230247,
        "sender_full_name": "Johan Commelin",
        "timestamp": 1773397046
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"112680\">Johan Commelin</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Logical.20system.20behind.20Lean.20diffs/near/579230247\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the \"Declarations diff\" [...]  will soon be computed deterministically using Lean, instead of grepping through the git diff.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would be great - I've been waiting for this for <em>years</em>!</p>",
        "id": 579230654,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1773397172
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's only tangentially related since it targets this kind of problem in the more general context of version control but maybe you're also interested in patch theory and <a href=\"https://pijul.com/manual/why_pijul.html\">pijul</a></p>",
        "id": 579239229,
        "sender_full_name": "Julian Berman",
        "timestamp": 1773399616
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Many thanks for sharing this <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"321696\">@Julian Berman</span></p>",
        "id": 579246689,
        "sender_full_name": "Joseph Tooby-Smith",
        "timestamp": 1773402036
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Somewhat related to my original message above: It seems that it is very hard on GitHub (for git non-experts) to implement a spilt of a pull-request into two separate parts. So that, for example, a reviewer could suggest certain changes be moved to a dependent or independent PR  and this be done in a button click.</p>",
        "id": 579247646,
        "sender_full_name": "Joseph Tooby-Smith",
        "timestamp": 1773402367
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's hard for git experts too :)</p>",
        "id": 579253725,
        "sender_full_name": "Julian Berman",
        "timestamp": 1773404485
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"653351\">Joseph Tooby-Smith</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Logical.20system.20behind.20Lean.20diffs/near/579247646\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It seems that it is very hard on GitHub (for git non-experts) to implement a spilt of a pull-request into two separate parts. So that, for example, a reviewer could suggest certain changes be moved to a dependent or independent PR  and this be done in a button click.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For mathlib, there is the <code>splice-bot</code> now. Otherwise  on a fresh branch <code>git checkout &lt;pr-branch&gt; &lt;file-path&gt;</code> is quite practical (when in the files changed view on github, the file path can be directly copied by clicking on the \"copy file path\" button).</p>",
        "id": 579255226,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1773404973
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Nice! Thanks <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"648495\">@Christian Merten</span>.</p>",
        "id": 579262584,
        "sender_full_name": "Joseph Tooby-Smith",
        "timestamp": 1773407005
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thinking about whether this is something we want this for PhysLib here <a href=\"#narrow/channel/479953-PhysLib/topic/Do.20we.20want.20.60splice-bot.60\">#PhysLib &gt; Do we want &#96;splice-bot&#96;</a></p>",
